<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Confederation of the Equator</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Confederation of the Equator was a short-lived republican and separatist revolt in northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1824, centred on Pernambuco. It arose in opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Emperor Pedro I</a>'s centralising constitution. The revolt was suppressed, but it showed the turbulence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a>'s founding decade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-equator.jpg" alt="Confederation of the Equator" width="320" height="134" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leandro Martins · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confederacao_equador_1824_exercito_imperial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The dissolution of the Brazilian Constituent Assembly in 1823 was badly received in Pernambuco. Its liberal leaders, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-de-carvalho-pais-de-andrade">Manuel de Carvalho Pais de Andrade</a> and the friar Joaquim do Amor Divino Rabelo e Caneca, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frei-caneca">Frei Caneca</a>, were republicans pardoned after the revolt of 1817, who had accepted the monarchy hoping for provincial autonomy. On 13 December 1823 the liberals forced the emperor's appointed president, Francisco Paes Barreto, to resign and illegally elected Pais de Andrade in his place, and they refused both Paes Barreto's return and a compromise president, José Carlos Mayrink da Silva Ferrão.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A naval division under John Taylor blockaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a> from April 1824, but rumours of a Portuguese naval attack drew it away, and on 2 July, a day after it left, Pais de Andrade declared Pernambuco independent and invited the northern and northeastern provinces to join a Confederation of the Equator. None did, apart from a few villages in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceara">Ceará</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraiba">Paraíba</a>. In Ceará the president was deposed in favour of the confederate Tristão Gonçalves de Alencar Araripe, but Fortaleza soon reaffirmed its loyalty, and in Pernambuco the rebels held little beyond Recife and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olinda">Olinda</a>, recruiting even children and old men by force.</p><p>In August the emperor sent Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cochrane-10th-earl-of-dundonald">Thomas Cochrane</a>'s squadron and 1,200 soldiers under Brigadier General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-lima-e-silva">Francisco de Lima e Silva</a>, who landed at Maceió and marched overland, gathering about 3,500 men. They attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a> on 12 September; Pais de Andrade fled secretly on a British ship, and the leaderless rebels were defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olinda">Olinda</a> five days later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frei-caneca">Frei Caneca</a> and a few others escaped towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceara">Ceará</a> but were defeated weeks later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Prosecutions ran from October 1824 to April 1825. Of the hundreds who had taken part in the three provinces, sixteen were condemned to death, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frei-caneca">Frei Caneca</a>. Sentenced to be hanged, he was shot instead because no executioner would hang him, on 13 January 1825, outside the walls of the Forte de São Tiago das Cinco Pontas. Pedro I pardoned the others on 7 March 1825.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Confederation of the Equator” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Confederation of the Equator</b> (1824) challenged imperial authority in the north-east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
